    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters August 25th with a clear hierarchy of efficiency, led by **Corbin Carroll** (73.39) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (72.92). **Corbin Carroll** remains a top-tier start despite facing Clay Holmes; his elite speed profile forces high-value outcomes even against challenging arms, and the +8% boost from the expected #2 order slot cements his status as our primary engine. Meanwhile, **Vinnie Pasquantino** draws the most favorable matchup of the day against Max Scherzer. With an xERA of 5.31 for the opposing starter and a significant dynamic platoon advantage (+14%), **Vinnie Pasquantino** is our highest-projected source of counting stats, particularly RBI and batting average stability. **Seiya Suzuki** also warrants attention at 65.61; while Brandon Pfaadt presents a moderate challenge, the aggressive baserunning potential against a \"slow delivery\" profile adds meaningful upside to his floor.\n\nThe most difficult decision for the lineup optimizer was leaving **Austin Riley** on the bench. With a score of 42.28, **Austin Riley** sat right on the edge of our \"Marginal\" threshold, but a tough matchup against Tyler Glasnow\u2014which neutralized his expected production by 19%\u2014ultimately relegated him to the pine. We are prioritizing players like **Isaac Paredes** (60.65) and **Gabriel Moreno** (60.37), who benefit from cleaner platoon alignments and safer volume. We are also keeping a close watch on **Jose Caballero** (58.59); despite a lower batting order position, he remains a critical component of our stolen base strategy, though his value is volatile today due to the platoon disadvantage. \n\nManagers should note that all assessments remain contingent on the final lineup cards. **Max Muncy** (55.14) and **Travis Bazzana** (54.39) occupy the lower end of our active starters; while they provide steady exposure to the lineup, their efficiency is dampened by either late-order placement or tough SP matchups. **Zach McKinstry** (44.73) is our most \"marginal\" play of the day; he is starting primarily to maintain our volume in the SB category, though he faces a daunting task against the 3.21 xERA of Ian Seymour. We are also monitoring the IL status of **James Wood**, who remains sidelined and unavailable for the immediate outlook. As always, the \"Lineup Pending\" tag on **Gabriel Moreno**, **Vinnie Pasquantino**, **Brice Turang**, and others creates a minor risk of sub-optimal order shifts before first pitch.",
